  "worked_solution": "First, notice that graph III resembles a straight line. So, we disregard this option.\n\nNow, remember that for $x>0,$ the graph of $y=-{x}^{3}$ decreases very rapidly as we increase $x.$ We say that $y\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ The only graphs that exhibit this decreasing property are I and II.\n\nNow, we need to choose between graphs I and II. To do this, let's evaluate $y=-{x}^{3}$ at some value of $x,$ say $x=0.$ Substituting $x=0$ into the function, we get\n\n$$\ny=-(0)^{3}=0,\n$$\n\nso the correct graph must pass through the point $(0,0).$ Only graph II satisfies this criterion.\n\nTherefore, graph II corresponds to the graph of $y=-{x}^{3}.$",
